Optional: the chip also flips between held back and "feet sink ~5 cm" at the same apex. To stop that, change engine.js:745 to prefer the held-back push on near-ties (`r.p > best.p * (1 - 1e-6)`), so that groundFails no longer flickers in.

In /home/user/dino-strength-sim/src/app.html, in renderLadder() draw(), add these lines right after the label-row rect at line 1238 (`g2.append(sv('rect', { x: left ? 0 : axisX + 9, ... }))`). Keep pointer-events:none on the .ring circle.

      // dot hit target: own half of the axis band, split at the midpoint to the nearest dot on the same side
      { const ys = (left ? tierItems : thItems).map((o) => y(o.lg)).sort((a, b) => a - b), k = ys.indexOf(yy);
        const top = Math.max(yy - 9, k > 0 ? (ys[k - 1] + yy) / 2 : -Infinity), bot = Math.min(yy + 9, k < ys.length - 1 ? (yy + ys[k + 1]) / 2 : Infinity);
        g2.append(sv('rect', { x: left ? axisX - 9 : axisX, y: top, width: 9, height: bot - top, fill: 'transparent' })); }

How it behaves:
- **One target per dot.** The rect sits inside the marker's own g2, so click, pointerenter, focus and keyboard all reach that marker. It fills the gap in the band that the label rects (now stopping at axisX±9) leave, so nothing overlaps it.
- **Dots close together.** Dots on opposite sides split left half and right half. Dots on the same side split at their midpoint. This covers Strongest human vs Captain America (3.3 px apart) and Secret Wars vs Hiroshima (1 px apart, opposite sides).

Then rebuild dist with `node /home/user/dino-strength-sim/test/shots.js`. I tested this change on a patched copy of dist at 1280 (mouse) and 390 (touch): all 18 dots set their own strength.

Three small edits in src/app.html, then rebuild. All three were verified in a patched scratch copy across 280 states.

1. logBars ticks (line 643): space ticks by the width of the widest label, and allow a single interval:
   const tickW = 6.6 * Math.max(...Array.from({ length: hi - lo + 1 }, (_, i) => unitFmt(Math.pow(10, lo + i)).length)) + 10;
   const maxTicks = Math.max(1, Math.floor((w - padL - padR) / tickW));
   Optional: also stack bar labels when w < 480 (`const stack = want > w * 0.45 || w < 480;`) so the sprint bars get about 236 px instead of 124.

2. nb() (line 471): bind a number to the next word only when the pair is short (a real unit), so that years and long values can wrap at the space:
   const nb = (t) => String(t).replace(/([\d.,]*\d) ([A-Za-zµ°%×]\S*)/g, (m, n, u) => n + (n.length + u.length < 10 ? ' ' : ' ') + u);
   With this, "1883 Krakatoa" and "2,500,000 m/s" can wrap at the space, while "1.06 kN", "10 Gt" and "20 km/s" stay joined. U+2060 inside "m/s" alone does not fix it, because the nbsp still joins the number and unit.

3. trajectoryChart (lines 606-607): label the origin "0", using `i === 0 ? '0' : fmtDist(yy)` and `i === 0 ? '0' : fmtDist(xx)`. This is safer than changing fmtDist(0) globally, because prose such as "feet sink …" also uses fmtDist.

In app.html bayThrow (line ~798), pass an end-of-path note for flights that never come down:
  const endNote = (f.landed || f.timedOut) ? null : (f.orbit ? 'in orbit →' : 'leaves Earth →');
  out.push(trajectoryChart(f.path, ghost, 'Flight path of the ' + o.short, f.apex, endNote));
In trajectoryChart(path, ghost, label, apexH, endNote), draw the apex dot and 'top …' text only when endNote is null. Otherwise, label the last point:
  if (endNote) svg.append(sv('text', { x: sx(last[0]) + 8, y: Math.max(sy(last[1]) - 8, padT + 10), 'text-anchor': sx(last[0]) > w - 150 ? 'end' : 'start', class: 'lbl-strong' }, endNote));
  else { /* existing apex circle + 'top ' label */ }
Optionally, add a caption under the chart for these cases: "Drawn up to 150 km, the top of the atmosphere; past that it follows its drag-free path away." The simpler alternative is to skip the chart entirely when f.escaped || f.orbit.

In trajectoryChart (src/app.html:593-599), size padL to the widest y label once Y is known. Drop padL from the first const line and add one line right after `const X = nice(maxX * 1.02), Y = nice(maxY * 1.05);`:

  const padL = Math.max(40, Math.ceil(Math.max(...[0, 1, 2, 3, 4].map((i) => fmtDist(Y * i / 4).length)) * 6.6) + 12);

sx and sy are defined after this line, so they pick up the new value. 6.6 px per character matches 11px IBM Plex Mono, and the extra 12 px covers the 6 px gap plus margin. The longest label fmtDist can produce is 10 characters ("750,000 km"); scientific labels for 1e9 m and beyond are up to about 12 characters. Rebuild dist with build-data.py or the usual build step, then re-run test/shots.js. Optionally, make fmtDist(0) return "0" so the origin no longer reads "0 mm".

In /home/user/dino-strength-sim/src/engine.js, pushApartHill (lines 232-240), replace the 200-step bisection with a bracketed Illinois solve. It gives the same results (within 1.6e-11 relative) for about one eighth of the evaluations:

      // solve g(S) = s2(S) - S = 0 for the new relative speed S in [0, vmax]. g falls with S, so
      // keep a bracket and step by Illinois false position, then bisect (g can jump where his
      // feet start to slide), to adjacent doubles.
      const g = (S) => { const F = force(S); return stepV(vA, F, RAof(F), cA, mA, aFree, dt) + stepV(vB, F, RB, cB, mB, bFree, dt) - S; };
      let lo = 0, hi = vmax, glo = g(0), ghi = g(vmax), side = 0;
      if (!(ghi < 0)) lo = vmax;
      else if (glo > 0) for (let i = 1; i <= 200; i++) {
        let S = i >= 8 ? 0.5 * (lo + hi) : (lo * ghi - hi * glo) / (ghi - glo);
        if (!(S > lo && S < hi)) S = 0.5 * (lo + hi);
        if (S <= lo || S >= hi) break;
        const gs = g(S);
        if (gs >= 0) { lo = S; glo = gs; if (side === 1) ghi *= 0.5; side = 1; }
        else { hi = S; ghi = gs; if (side === -1) glo *= 0.5; side = -1; }
        if (gs === 0 || hi - lo <= 4e-16 * hi) break;
      }

Keep the following `const F = force(lo);` line as it is. The `gs === 0` stop and the `>=` comparison are both needed. Without them a hit on the exact root either leaves a stale `lo` (a 3% speed error on a baseball) or falls into needless bisection.

The tested copy is scratchpad/audit3/n6v/eng-final.js. Rebuild the page, then rerun test/calibration.js and the throw sweep. Don't cut the cap list or the comic-to-real rerun to save time, because those change results.

Optional: coalescing strength-slider renders with requestAnimationFrame or a short timeout, as scheduleBay does, only stops key-repeat events queueing up. It doesn't remove the per-render cost.
